 "standard_url": "https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/sboe-rules-tac/sboe-tac-currently-in-effect/ch111c.pdf",
 "standard_text": "A.10C determine the quotient of a polynomial of degree one and polynomial of degree two when divided by a polynomial of degree one and polynomial of degree two when the degree of the divisor does not exceed the degree of the dividend",
 "meta_desc": "Free Algebra I worksheets for Texas TEKS A.10C: divide polynomials by monomials and binomial factors, find missing factors and solve area problems. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Factor the dividend, then cancel the divisor.",
 "key_sub": "(x² + 7x + 12) ÷ (x + 3) = x + 4",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>To divide by a monomial, divide <b>every term</b>: (6<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 9<i>x</i>) ÷ 3<i>x</i> = 2<i>x</i> + 3.</li><li>To divide by a binomial factor, <b>factor</b> the dividend and cancel the common factor.</li><li>Check: quotient × divisor = dividend.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>(<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 7<i>x</i> + 12) ÷ (<i>x</i> + 3)</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>(<i>x</i> + 3)(<i>x</i> + 4)</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> + 4</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dividing only the first term</td><td>Every term is divided.</td></tr><tr><td>Keeping the wrong sign</td><td>Check by multiplying.</td></tr><tr><td>Adding the constants</td><td>Factor, then cancel.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does TEKS A.10C ask?",
   "a": "Determine the quotient of a polynomial of degree one and polynomial of degree two when divided by a polynomial of degree one and polynomial of degree two when the degree of the divisor does not exceed the degree of the dividend."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I check a division?",
   "a": "Multiply the quotient by the divisor."
  },
  {
   "q": "What if the divisor is not a factor?",
   "a": "On these worksheets it always is."
  },
  {
   "q": "Can I use long division?",
   "a": "Yes, but factoring is often faster."
  }
 ],
